From 22ab3df6d124b002eb626b290a687a7f2004651d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 01:22:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Grow the property map's trie geometrically from one node
 group

VirtualMemory.Allocate reserved 16 slots for the first request of any
size. A SAUint32Map node is 56 bytes, so that made the FIRST property of
any object cost 920 B of trie it did not use, and made one field cost
exactly the same as three.

Measured over Octane 2.0 with --property-map-distribution (item 2-7's
measurement, patch 0057): across 47 482 058 property maps, 43.9% end
their life at ONE four-node group and 87.3% never leave the old floor.
The reservation was almost never used. Both shares are converged -
tripling the sample moves them by hundredths of a point, and only suites
with a few hundred maps move at all.

Replaced with geometric growth from the smallest useful size. Against
that distribution: live map bytes 0.56x, allocated 0.82x. Measured rather
than modelled - changing the floor and re-running --object-alloc moves
ctor-1 from 1 256.5 B to 584.4 B, a 672.1 B saving against the predicted
920 -> 248 = 672, with the 120 B of non-map overhead identical in both
builds.

The item this comes from described the old floor as "buying amortized
growth for medium objects with memory that small objects do not use".
That is not what it did. `((max / 16) + 1) * 16` only applies while
`last * 2 <= max`, so past the first block the rule grew by a FIXED
INCREMENT - linearly - and paid more copies than doubling, not fewer. It
was not buying anything for medium objects; it was overcharging small
ones. Which is why the replacement wins on memory AND time.

A real trade with a real losing side: an eight-field object pays about
27% more bytes and ~19% more time, because it now resizes and copies
where it used to fit. Taken because the tail is 12.7% of maps and the
real suites confirm it. Whole-process wall clock, two interleaved rounds:
Typescript 0.916, Box2D 0.937, PdfJS 1.013, EarleyBoyer 1.020 -
Typescript has by far the worst tail, a third of its maps outgrowing the
old floor, and it is the suite that gains most, which is the geometric
half paying for the smaller-floor half. Small-object loops run at
0.73-0.85x; a hot property read and a local-arithmetic loop are flat at
1.01x.

Also fixes two flaws in 0057's own emitter, found by reading its output
rather than trusting it: the histogram produced negative buckets because
resetting part-way through a map's life decremented a bucket the map was
never counted in (the window now spans the load, which is also the more
honest denominator), and Octane's zlib died on a missing `print`/`read`
instead of running.

5 test cases in StorageTests: the first allocation reserving exactly what
was asked, a sub-group request still getting a whole group, growth being
geometric rather than a fixed increment, every slot's contents surviving
50 growths - the policy is only safe because a resize copies - and
SAUint32Map keeping all 2 000 entries across the resizes it now forces.

Suite: 7 397 tests across 13 projects, 0 failures.

Refs docs/performance-roadmap.md item 2-7.
